Oh, total amounts stored.

230
00:14:11,180 --> 00:14:12,180
Yeah.

231
00:14:12,180 --> 00:14:15,860
TVL is I'm trying to think in the 80 billion range, maybe.

232
00:14:15,860 --> 00:14:16,860
No, sorry.

233
00:14:16,860 --> 00:14:17,860
80 billion might be Aave.

234
00:14:17,860 --> 00:14:19,500
So it's probably double that.

235
00:14:19,660 --> 00:14:20,660
I looked at it.

236
00:14:20,660 --> 00:14:21,660
It's 150 billion.

237
00:14:21,660 --> 00:14:25,740
So if you think about 11 billion of that being stolen is insane.

238
00:14:25,740 --> 00:14:28,540
That's nearly 10% of all of our TVL.

239
00:14:28,540 --> 00:14:30,860
And by the way, 2025, we've gone up a lot.

240
00:14:30,860 --> 00:14:35,340
It used to be 40 billion in TVL just a few years ago.

241
00:14:35,340 --> 00:14:37,540
So it's a higher percentage on that basis.

242
00:14:37,540 --> 00:14:38,540
Way higher.

243
00:14:38,540 --> 00:14:43,340
So I think in my time being around the ecosystem, the number of hacks have gone up, the amount

244
00:14:43,340 --> 00:14:46,460
being hacked has gone up by 1.5 billion, I think.

245
00:14:46,460 --> 00:14:50,580
That comes from the fact that there's more TVL being held, adversaries are getting much

246
00:14:50,580 --> 00:14:55,460
smarter, AI is being adopted now to hack code bases as well.

247
00:14:55,460 --> 00:14:58,660
And teams really need to start thinking about security for first principles.

248
00:14:58,660 --> 00:15:03,540
That being said, today, compared to maybe two years ago when I started Octane, I've

249
00:15:03,540 --> 00:15:09,020
seen a dramatic shift in terms of the way that these teams are handling security that

250
00:15:09,020 --> 00:15:10,220
we're working with.

251
00:15:10,220 --> 00:15:15,140
Protocol teams, infrastructure teams, L1s, they're all really thinking about security

252
00:15:15,140 --> 00:15:18,940
from a mature perspective for the most part, which is really encouraging.

253
00:15:18,940 --> 00:15:23,140
So I think the threat vectors are going up, but our responses to those threat factors

254
00:15:23,140 --> 00:15:27,420
and our focus on pre-deployment security has also gone up as an ecosystem.

255
00:15:27,420 --> 00:15:31,300
From a builder's perspective, because you mentioned, I imagine you get varying levels

256
00:15:31,300 --> 00:15:37,420
of scenario where someone shipped a full product and, okay, I need to do security now, or they

257
00:15:37,420 --> 00:15:41,340
have a launch in two weeks and, okay, I need to make sure nothing bad happens.

258
00:15:41,340 --> 00:15:42,980
That's better than not doing that.

259
00:15:43,300 --> 00:15:46,860
In an ideal world, obviously, security is a day one thought, which I think that a lot

260
00:15:46,860 --> 00:15:50,780
of thoughtful founders make that decision, but today I don't think we're collectively

261
00:15:50,780 --> 00:15:52,460
at that state yet.

262
00:15:52,460 --> 00:15:56,300
How important is it that security is a day one thought?

263
00:15:56,300 --> 00:16:00,820
And how much do you see debt, whether it's operational debt, whether it's tech debt,

264
00:16:00,820 --> 00:16:04,540
because people haven't been security focused from day one and they've already done this

265
00:16:04,540 --> 00:16:05,540
build out?

266
00:16:05,540 --> 00:16:10,620
The biggest full case I can give to this for any founder listening is it will be cheaper

267
00:16:10,700 --> 00:16:14,740
for you to approach this from day one, even though you don't think it is.

268
00:16:14,740 --> 00:16:18,380
What we see is a lot of founders will just wait until they're ready for deployment and

269
00:16:18,380 --> 00:16:20,260
be like, okay, now I got to do security.

270
00:16:20,260 --> 00:16:21,260
But here's what happens.

271
00:16:21,260 --> 00:16:26,420
They get one big manual audit that costs them somewhere between $50,000 and $200,000.

272
00:16:26,420 --> 00:16:32,100
The manual audit comes back with a ton of highs and criticals, so your security profile

273
00:16:32,100 --> 00:16:33,860
on chain is just terrible.

274
00:16:33,860 --> 00:16:38,460
You have a lot of different things to fix because you didn't start thinking about this

275
00:16:38,580 --> 00:16:40,300
until you're right about to deploy.

276
00:16:40,300 --> 00:16:41,900
Okay, so now what happens?

277
00:16:41,900 --> 00:16:46,260
It's going to be a couple of weeks before you fix all of that and get all the audit

278
00:16:46,260 --> 00:16:48,060
process actually wrapped up.

279
00:16:48,060 --> 00:16:51,020
And then you're probably going to need to get another manual audit for a couple of reasons.

280
00:16:51,020 --> 00:16:54,520
One, you don't want just one, but two, if you had that many highs and criticals, there's

281
00:16:54,520 --> 00:16:55,860
probably more.

282
00:16:55,860 --> 00:17:01,840
And so now you're at somewhere between $100,000 and $400,000 in security spend.

283
00:17:01,840 --> 00:17:06,020
And then you're going to wrap up that process and then maybe you'll do one more.

284
00:17:06,020 --> 00:17:09,420
Hopefully you don't have a ton of highs and criticals after that second one, and then

285
00:17:09,420 --> 00:17:10,540
you'll get ready to deploy.

286
00:17:10,540 --> 00:17:13,500
But this whole process is now taking you multiple months.

287
00:17:13,500 --> 00:17:18,220
So now you're in a worse spot because if you start security from day one, you will have

288
00:17:18,220 --> 00:17:23,940
AI and automated tooling catching a significant amount of those vulnerabilities early upfront

289
00:17:23,940 --> 00:17:25,260
continuously.

290
00:17:25,260 --> 00:17:28,740
So when you go for those spot checks, those manual audits, hopefully your vulnerability

291
00:17:28,740 --> 00:17:32,340
count will be extremely low because you're already aware of a lot of the potential risks

292
00:17:32,340 --> 00:17:35,780
that you've been introducing in your continuous development pipeline.

293
00:17:35,780 --> 00:17:39,300
And then that process speeds up, remediation time is faster.

294
00:17:39,300 --> 00:17:42,740
The number of audits you'll have to do is hopefully lower.

295
00:17:42,740 --> 00:17:46,820
And overall, this is how we get to help teams save money on security.

296
00:17:46,820 --> 00:17:53,300
And I think the security ecosystem at the moment up to today or up to maybe 2025 has

297
00:17:53,300 --> 00:17:57,900
been so focused on manual audits and doing that process that I mentioned and has spent

298
00:17:57,900 --> 00:18:02,980
so much money on this that it has been inaccessible to a lot of builders.

299
00:18:03,140 --> 00:18:08,580
My hope is that with Octane and with automation of pre-deployment security and point in time

300
00:18:08,580 --> 00:18:14,580
manual checks that are surgical, we're going to enable more people to build in crypto because

301
00:18:14,580 --> 00:18:16,500
security becomes more accessible.

302
00:18:16,500 --> 00:18:18,540
So yeah, that's my long ramble on that.

303
00:18:18,540 --> 00:18:20,660
No, I think that's a really important point.

304
00:18:20,660 --> 00:18:22,820
And we see this from the venture side.

305
00:18:22,820 --> 00:18:26,780
Teams that don't pay for security for the first part of their build because they can't

306
00:18:26,780 --> 00:18:30,580
afford it because it's a very all or nothing offering or process.

307
00:18:30,580 --> 00:18:33,540
And because of that, you're in this debt situation you're talking about.

308
00:18:33,540 --> 00:18:38,020
One thing I'll add to this, this morning, Bunny Protocol, which got hacked, they had

309
00:18:38,020 --> 00:18:39,020
to shut down.

310
00:18:39,020 --> 00:18:40,020
They posted this on Twitter.

311
00:18:40,020 --> 00:18:43,060
They said, we're not going to be able to relaunch.

312
00:18:43,060 --> 00:18:46,580
We've looked at the costs of doing the manual audits to go live again.

313
00:18:46,580 --> 00:18:49,420
They said six to seven figures from their tweet.

314
00:18:49,420 --> 00:18:50,420
That is insane.

315
00:18:50,420 --> 00:18:52,740
There are ways to, I think, avoid that.

316
00:18:52,740 --> 00:18:56,580
That being said, Bunny Protocol is incredibly complex as Uniswap v4 hooks.

317
00:18:56,580 --> 00:19:01,780
It is going to be an intensive security process regardless, but I do think that leveraging

318
00:19:01,780 --> 00:19:07,020
automation and using AI infrastructure and tooling that way will hopefully bring some

319
00:19:07,020 --> 00:19:08,780
of those costs down in the future.

320
00:19:08,780 --> 00:19:12,580
And also we have been able to catch a lot of vulnerabilities that were missed in manual

321
00:19:12,580 --> 00:19:13,580
reviews.

322
00:19:13,580 --> 00:19:18,340
So hopefully from a security risk perspective, Octane and similar types of infrastructure

323
00:19:18,340 --> 00:19:20,820
can enable better risk profiles.

324
00:19:20,820 --> 00:19:26,520
As we talk about costs and operational security practices, I'm curious for your view, as

325
00:19:26,520 --> 00:19:30,700
especially with your founder hat on, you've raised funds to your budgeting.

326
00:19:30,700 --> 00:19:34,960
Do you have a benchmark for how much teams should be spending on security relative to

327
00:19:34,960 --> 00:19:37,920
their budget, relative to the amount of funding they have?

328
00:19:37,920 --> 00:19:39,120
That's a good question.

329
00:19:39,120 --> 00:19:44,260
I think it really depends on what your risk profile looks like.

330
00:19:44,260 --> 00:19:49,080
And by that, I mean, is your protocol going to be handling lots of dollars in volume of

331
00:19:49,080 --> 00:19:53,600
assets or is it going to not maybe handle a lot of digital assets?

332
00:19:53,600 --> 00:19:56,280
And depending on that, I might have different answers.

333
00:19:56,280 --> 00:20:00,480
But I think because most teams in crypto are falling in the bucket of handling a lot

334
00:20:00,480 --> 00:20:05,240
of user funds, I would recommend that if you do it right, I think you can budget somewhere

335
00:20:05,240 --> 00:20:10,120
between maybe 25 to 100K a year, somewhere in that range, depending on also how much

336
00:20:10,120 --> 00:20:11,760
code you're planning to deploy.

337
00:20:11,760 --> 00:20:15,920
I think infrastructure and tooling automation, some is free, some is paid.

338
00:20:15,920 --> 00:20:20,080
I think you should definitely bake that into your budget because I think it will save money

339
00:20:20,080 --> 00:20:22,080
on the overall budget and increase security.

340
00:20:22,080 --> 00:20:26,200
And you should also budget for putting up bug bounties, getting manual audits done,

341
00:20:26,200 --> 00:20:27,680
getting audit competitions done.

342
00:20:27,680 --> 00:20:31,740
I think depending on your cadence of code deployment, you could get that all done within

343
00:20:31,740 --> 00:20:33,160
that range a year.

344
00:20:33,160 --> 00:20:39,120
For most startup founders, and maybe the BC to Series A range, what we've seen is as teams

345
00:20:39,120 --> 00:20:45,600
start to scale up in size and protocols grow, we see an increase in volume of the on-chain

346
00:20:45,600 --> 00:20:46,600
products being deployed.

347
00:20:46,600 --> 00:20:50,560
And so that should equate to an increase in volume on security spent.

348
00:20:50,560 --> 00:20:54,560
I think it's important for investors, whether it's funds or angels to be aware of too, because

349
00:20:54,600 --> 00:20:59,560
if you want to invest in a protocol and maybe it's a small financing and they want to get

350
00:20:59,560 --> 00:21:03,680
a product to market quickly, you have to make sure that the security budget's in there.

351
00:21:03,680 --> 00:21:04,680
100%.

352
00:21:04,680 --> 00:21:06,920
The security budget definitely needs to be in there.

353
00:21:06,920 --> 00:21:11,320
But I think it doesn't need to be as crazy as it's been for the past few years.

354
00:21:11,320 --> 00:21:12,420
Do you want to touch on that?

355
00:21:12,420 --> 00:21:16,600
You feel like people have been forced to overspend on security in recent times?

356
00:21:16,600 --> 00:21:21,820
I've seen teams have massive budgets for security, and I think that's fantastic.

357
00:21:21,820 --> 00:21:24,120
I think it's really great that some teams can do that.

358
00:21:24,120 --> 00:21:27,400
I think Uniswap had a $20 million budget.

359
00:21:27,400 --> 00:21:32,320
I don't want to misquote, but I think that's it for their V4 hooks because they had a big

360
00:21:32,320 --> 00:21:33,320
audit competition.

361
00:21:33,320 --> 00:21:36,240
They had a big bug bounty, and then they had a bunch of manual audits done.

362
00:21:36,240 --> 00:21:40,120
And that code is being used to build a bunch of different protocols on top of it, so it's

363
00:21:40,120 --> 00:21:41,120
really important.

364
00:21:41,120 --> 00:21:45,240
But yeah, I think security spend has been very high over the past few years.

365
00:21:45,240 --> 00:21:48,920
I think it's because manual auditing prices are very, very high.

366
00:21:48,920 --> 00:21:54,080
I do think there's a lot of manual auditing firms that produce really, really good outputs.

367
00:21:54,080 --> 00:21:56,400
I think there's some that don't produce great outputs.

368
00:21:56,400 --> 00:22:01,520
I think it's about how you approach that process, which vendors you go with for that, and how

369
00:22:01,520 --> 00:22:05,840
you think about chopping up that budget across multiple parts of the pre-deployment security

370
00:22:05,840 --> 00:22:06,840
spend.

371
00:22:06,840 --> 00:22:08,840
Don't just get one manual audit, please.

372
00:22:08,840 --> 00:22:14,520
So to that point about security budgeting, how would you assess what is effective spend

373
00:22:14,520 --> 00:22:15,520
there?

374
00:22:15,520 --> 00:22:20,080
Because I imagine in some cases, maybe you'll have a heavily funded project and they know

375
00:22:20,240 --> 00:22:23,240
they need to spend $4 million on security.

376
00:22:23,240 --> 00:22:26,700
So they're going to go and try and spend $4 million.

377
00:22:26,700 --> 00:22:30,480
And I wonder from your view, there's any bearing their head in the sand if there are some things

378
00:22:30,480 --> 00:22:33,840
where, okay, there's a lot of spend here, whereas this would be important.

379
00:22:33,840 --> 00:22:36,800
How do you look at how that security budgeting is happening?

380
00:22:36,800 --> 00:22:40,600
And then what is valuable along that stack in your view?

381
00:22:40,600 --> 00:22:46,660
The way that I would stack it up is what you're paying for from your budget, actually leading

382
00:22:46,660 --> 00:22:51,100
to more security risks being discovered and at what rate.

383
00:22:51,100 --> 00:22:56,460
So I think tooling automation can discover a lot of security risk fairly on and continuously.

384
00:22:56,460 --> 00:23:01,380
I also think it can speed up some of your processes and audit remediations.

385
00:23:01,380 --> 00:23:07,260
So we've had customers that find vulnerabilities in the code changes that they're making themselves

386
00:23:07,260 --> 00:23:11,140
before they send those code changes back to auditors, and they can fix those security

387
00:23:11,140 --> 00:23:14,860
issues that they've introduced before sending it back to the auditors, which then saves

388
00:23:14,860 --> 00:23:16,060
them an extra cycle.

389
00:23:16,060 --> 00:23:21,340
So I think as long as tooling and infrastructure is producing a good amount of net new vulnerabilities

390
00:23:21,340 --> 00:23:26,740
for you, your manual audits are producing a good amount of new vulnerabilities that

391
00:23:26,740 --> 00:23:31,980
are difficult to find and that are correctly classified in severity, and you're understanding

392
00:23:31,980 --> 00:23:36,260
your operational risks with these security tools and manual reviews.

393
00:23:36,260 --> 00:23:39,400
And then you're also setting yourself up for success with bug bounties.

394
00:23:39,400 --> 00:23:43,340
I think that's a pretty good workflow to be able to budget effectively.

395
00:23:43,340 --> 00:23:45,740
Am I catching a lot of bugs with the money I'm spending?

396
00:23:45,740 --> 00:23:48,860
Am I building good processes in case things go wrong?

397
00:23:48,860 --> 00:23:53,620
You can also learn a lot about this by seeing how very seasoned teams approach this, like

398
00:23:53,620 --> 00:23:57,700
Uniswap or Circle, which are our customers, but yeah, I think they do this really, really

399
00:23:57,700 --> 00:23:58,700
well.

400
00:23:58,700 --> 00:24:04,300
And maybe branching off of producing good security practices for an organization themselves.

401
00:24:04,300 --> 00:24:09,620
In crypto, we have this ethos called composability, where you want things to work very well together.

402
00:24:09,620 --> 00:24:13,420
And from my perspective, one of the things that comes with that is a lot of counterparty

403
00:24:13,420 --> 00:24:19,220
risk in that the workflow of a given product might be you link your phantom wallet to

404
00:24:19,220 --> 00:24:23,660
deck screener, which runs Meteora in the background, which might be sourcing the equity from somewhere

405
00:24:23,660 --> 00:24:24,660
else.

406
00:24:24,660 --> 00:24:29,000
And you're sometimes using four or five different products at the same time and accompanying

407
00:24:29,000 --> 00:24:31,460
code bases could be more code bases.

408
00:24:31,460 --> 00:24:36,540
How do you talk with customers or people in the industry generally about balancing risk

409
00:24:36,540 --> 00:24:37,820
in that regard?

410
00:24:37,820 --> 00:24:43,060
Because if you are one product in that stack along that usage experience, a vulnerability

411
00:24:43,180 --> 00:24:48,020
or an exploit to one of those other accompanying products is really detrimental to you.

412
00:24:48,020 --> 00:24:52,380
So your question is, how do you think about managing risk when you have a lot of different

413
00:24:52,380 --> 00:24:53,900
composable products?

414
00:24:53,900 --> 00:24:54,900
Yeah.

415
00:24:54,900 --> 00:24:58,380
And when you're relying on other products in your own workflow?

416
00:24:58,380 --> 00:25:00,980
As an end user or as a business?

417
00:25:00,980 --> 00:25:03,300
As a business first, but then maybe as an end user.

418
00:25:03,300 --> 00:25:04,300
Yeah.

419
00:25:04,300 --> 00:25:08,340
So I think as a business first, what we recommend is every product that you're going to use

420
00:25:08,340 --> 00:25:12,940
and you're going to integrate with is a new place for vulnerability to be introduced and

421
00:25:12,940 --> 00:25:15,300
risk to your system to occur.

422
00:25:15,300 --> 00:25:19,820
So first thing that we also see a lot of teams doing it is they'll go through the manual

423
00:25:19,820 --> 00:25:21,740
audit reports of these teams.

424
00:25:21,740 --> 00:25:26,860
If somebody is going to integrate, let's say Curve or Aave into their system, whether it's

425
00:25:26,860 --> 00:25:30,980
their backend system that's routing orders or it's their on-chain protocol.

426
00:25:30,980 --> 00:25:34,620
If they're going to start doing this, read up on their documentation, look at how many

427
00:25:34,620 --> 00:25:35,900
audits they've got.

428
00:25:35,940 --> 00:25:42,380
A lot of the time, there are known risks that are noted in the documentation of these protocols.

429
00:25:42,380 --> 00:25:46,620
And we've seen a lot of customers have vulnerabilities introduced in those exact locations where

430
00:25:46,620 --> 00:25:49,340
people are calling cross-chain to another protocol.

431
00:25:49,340 --> 00:25:52,940
We've actually seen that be missed by a lot of manual auditors as well, because sometimes

432
00:25:52,940 --> 00:25:57,100
teams don't consider those instances, so they don't look as deeply into that.

433
00:25:57,100 --> 00:26:01,540
And we've caught vulnerabilities that were missed by manual reviews using Octane because

434
00:26:01,700 --> 00:26:06,260
compile all the dependencies and look at the source code flow across and into dependencies

435
00:26:06,260 --> 00:26:07,260
as well.

436
00:26:07,260 --> 00:26:10,980
Which is just funny, when to your point they've already been publicly reported.

437
00:26:10,980 --> 00:26:12,740
Yeah, but it's tricky.

438
00:26:12,740 --> 00:26:14,980
For engineers, there's a lot going on.

439
00:26:14,980 --> 00:26:18,980
There's a lot of different pieces to the puzzle, a lot of security they're trying to manage

440
00:26:18,980 --> 00:26:19,980
on their own side.

441
00:26:19,980 --> 00:26:24,300
So yeah, a lot of devs will look into the documentation of these protocols, but sometimes

442
00:26:24,300 --> 00:26:28,740
there can be risks introduced on those integration points a lot of the time, actually.

443
00:26:28,740 --> 00:26:31,820
So yeah, I think looking into the docs there, reading up on the source code that you're

444
00:26:31,820 --> 00:26:33,700
actually integrating with is really important.

445
00:26:33,700 --> 00:26:38,780
And then also for a business, I think making sure that all of those external touch points

446
00:26:38,780 --> 00:26:39,780
are managed.

447
00:26:39,780 --> 00:26:45,940
So you have some information feed to know if those protocols are hacked or exploited

448
00:26:45,940 --> 00:26:49,500
or there is a bug, and you make sure to keep active tabs on that.

449
00:26:49,500 --> 00:26:54,220
And there's companies that provide these types of information streams and also do on-chain

450
00:26:54,220 --> 00:26:55,220
monitoring.

451
00:26:55,220 --> 00:26:59,020
I think that's very helpful to get those alerts immediately, so you can pause everything

452
00:26:59,020 --> 00:27:03,140
on your protocol side, make sure your user funds don't get lost.

453
00:27:03,140 --> 00:27:05,940
And how about from the end user perspective, because I think you brought up an interesting

454
00:27:05,940 --> 00:27:06,940
point there.

455
00:27:06,940 --> 00:27:12,260
I personally try and go with the very simple heuristic of like, if I can use two apps or

456
00:27:12,260 --> 00:27:16,460
two platforms, as opposed to five, then I'll air that direction.

457
00:27:16,460 --> 00:27:19,060
But I'm curious, how do you look at it from the end user perspective?

458
00:27:19,060 --> 00:27:23,380
Yeah, I think that's helpful, minimizing the number of forms that you're using.

459
00:27:23,380 --> 00:27:24,980
I think that's really, really helpful.

460
00:27:24,980 --> 00:27:31,060
I also think you should definitely take into account how teams are handling security.

461
00:27:31,060 --> 00:27:35,140
And you can see this sometimes socially, but also in documentation too.

462
00:27:35,140 --> 00:27:37,420
Are they posting about their code reviews?

463
00:27:37,420 --> 00:27:40,380
Are they posting about tooling and infrastructure that they're integrating?

464
00:27:40,380 --> 00:27:41,740
That's certainly a pretty positive sign.

465
00:27:41,740 --> 00:27:45,500
If they're not, that's probably a red flag if they don't have any listed reports or anything

466
00:27:45,500 --> 00:27:46,500
like that.

467
00:27:46,500 --> 00:27:50,460
And then I also think as an end user, there's a lot of different things that can go wrong

468
00:27:50,460 --> 00:27:52,100
on the browser level.

469
00:27:52,100 --> 00:27:56,500
People can inject JavaScript into your computer and then you could end up processing a transaction

470
00:27:56,500 --> 00:28:00,340
you didn't expect, which is what happened with Bybit to a degree.

471
00:28:00,340 --> 00:28:04,440
So I also think that's really important, making sure you have concealed contained environments.

472
00:28:04,440 --> 00:28:07,180
You don't click on URLs that are phishing emails.

473
00:28:07,180 --> 00:28:10,580
And if you do, you make sure to wipe your computer clean of any malware that you might

474
00:28:10,580 --> 00:28:11,580
have downloaded.

475
00:28:11,580 --> 00:28:14,480
So I think all of that is really important on the end user side too.

476
00:28:14,480 --> 00:28:18,640
In that vein, because I did want to ask you from your vantage point, where are most exploits

477
00:28:18,640 --> 00:28:20,060
happening today?

478
00:28:20,060 --> 00:28:22,780
I think it's really around centralization risk.

479
00:28:22,780 --> 00:28:28,980
So I think it's around private keys that have access to privileged roles on code bases and

480
00:28:28,980 --> 00:28:34,160
misuse of those private keys or someone being able to acquire those private keys through

481
00:28:34,160 --> 00:28:35,580
social engineering.

482
00:28:35,580 --> 00:28:39,660
And I think that's actually where some of the biggest hacks happen, like Bybit for example.

483
00:28:39,660 --> 00:28:46,260
But also seven days ago, Axos accidentally minted $300 trillion of DYUSD.

484
00:28:46,260 --> 00:28:50,200
And I think that goes to show you centralization is a really important thing.

485
00:28:50,200 --> 00:28:52,580
And we also see this a lot with proxies.

486
00:28:52,580 --> 00:28:57,740
So we published a blog post recently on Octane about the risk of having upgradable proxies

487
00:28:57,740 --> 00:29:03,060
and private keys that control those upgradable proxies, how to manage security around that

488
00:29:03,060 --> 00:29:05,660
and think about multi-signature wallets.

489
00:29:05,660 --> 00:29:06,980
There's a bunch of things that we wrote in there.

490
00:29:06,980 --> 00:29:11,340
I think that's a really good deep dive into how to manage centralization risk, especially

491
00:29:11,340 --> 00:29:13,300
around proxy code bases.

492
00:29:13,300 --> 00:29:17,180
The proxies are really useful because if there is a hack or an exploit, you know, the ability

493
00:29:17,180 --> 00:29:23,380
hopefully if you set up a proxy right to upgrade the functionality and avoid the exploit continuing,

494
00:29:23,380 --> 00:29:24,860
but it does come with its own risks.

495
00:29:24,860 --> 00:29:28,140
So that goes back to my thing that I said earlier too about understanding where all

496
00:29:28,140 --> 00:29:30,680
of your risk is and why you're taking it that way.

497
00:29:30,680 --> 00:29:31,860
I like your point.

498
00:29:31,860 --> 00:29:37,740
I think these individual behavioral, private key behaviors, centralization around one person

499
00:29:38,740 --> 00:29:44,660
access is to your point, a threat vector right now that a doubt I've for a long time thought

500
00:29:44,660 --> 00:29:51,420
there's room for what I'd call almost an on-chain notary business in that you would have someone

501
00:29:51,420 --> 00:29:55,900
if I want to sign something or if I want to issue a transaction, they would almost be

502
00:29:55,900 --> 00:29:59,100
a fail safe and be like, Hey, I don't like what you just signed.

503
00:29:59,100 --> 00:30:04,140
Or in the case of Bybit, that behavior requires almost sign off from an impartial notary who's

504
00:30:04,140 --> 00:30:07,940
I don't know if I call it a trusted party or a sound one.

505
00:30:07,940 --> 00:30:11,540
And I'm almost surprised that hasn't emerged yet because I think we see a lot of these

506
00:30:11,540 --> 00:30:14,220
rush the moment type exploits.

507
00:30:14,220 --> 00:30:16,380
I think it munifies actually doing this now.

508
00:30:16,380 --> 00:30:17,540
And I think it's a great offering.

509
00:30:17,540 --> 00:30:18,540
I think so.

510
00:30:18,540 --> 00:30:24,180
But basically there's security companies now that are acting as third party intermediaries

511
00:30:24,180 --> 00:30:30,660
that can advise on multi-signature transactions or movement of funds and upgradability of

512
00:30:30,660 --> 00:30:31,660
proxies.

513
00:30:31,660 --> 00:30:32,660
And I think that's really, really good.

514
00:30:32,860 --> 00:30:35,540
I think the crypto ecosystem really needs that.

515
00:30:35,540 --> 00:30:40,660
So I do think that's starting to become a product and offering and service that more

516
00:30:40,660 --> 00:30:42,780
of these security companies are offering.

517
00:30:42,780 --> 00:30:44,300
And I think it's great.

518
00:30:44,300 --> 00:30:49,300
And one of the main criticisms I hear from let's call it Wall Street is that you need

519
00:30:49,300 --> 00:30:54,060
reversibility, which I don't know if crypto can ever have true reversibility, but I think

520
00:30:54,060 --> 00:31:00,180
the ability to almost decline bad transactions that were initiated by someone is a good medium

521
00:31:00,180 --> 00:31:02,260
ground, at least for the time being.

522
00:31:02,260 --> 00:31:03,660
It's another security risk.

523
00:31:03,660 --> 00:31:04,660
100%.

524
00:31:04,660 --> 00:31:08,620
Somebody to then reverse the chain and then do a whole bunch of stuff.

525
00:31:08,620 --> 00:31:13,020
A lot of the time that stuff does happen.

526
00:31:13,020 --> 00:31:17,220
Chains do enable that on the chain level, depending on the scenario.

527
00:31:17,220 --> 00:31:22,620
And I think the fork from Ethereum versus Ethereum Classic was based on a reorg of that

528
00:31:22,620 --> 00:31:25,100
nature to back back, I think.

529
00:31:25,100 --> 00:31:32,180
But yeah, I do think that being able to have reversibility is great from a user perspective

530
00:31:32,380 --> 00:31:36,980
but it also is not so great because it means that there's a centralized entity that can

531
00:31:36,980 --> 00:31:38,980
effectively undo transactions.

532
00:31:38,980 --> 00:31:44,020
It makes sense for Wall Street and bigger enterprises to have that concern.

533
00:31:44,020 --> 00:31:48,500
And for some of them, I think private blockchains and different types of blockchains that can

534
00:31:48,500 --> 00:31:50,860
provide solutions like that would be useful.

535
00:31:50,860 --> 00:31:51,860
Agreed.

536
00:31:51,860 --> 00:31:55,660
I'm going to circle back to talk about Octane in a little bit more detail.

537
00:31:55,660 --> 00:31:59,460
I wanted to touch on first, you mentioned how you guys are sitting at the pre-deployment

538
00:31:59,460 --> 00:32:00,460
stage.

539
00:32:00,460 --> 00:32:02,100
Who do you like for post-deployment?

540
00:32:02,100 --> 00:32:07,380
Is there anyone that you prescribe for customers or that you are partial to?

541
00:32:07,380 --> 00:32:11,340
Yeah, we've definitely worked with a bunch of post-deployment providers.

542
00:32:11,340 --> 00:32:15,300
I think that teams should go out and evaluate all providers.

543
00:32:15,300 --> 00:32:21,340
But I'd say we've seen some great work from Guardrail as well as Hypernative and Hexagate,

544
00:32:21,340 --> 00:32:22,340
a few others.

545
00:32:22,340 --> 00:32:24,820
But I would definitely recommend checking them all out.

546
00:32:24,820 --> 00:32:28,820
And I think they all have their own unique edge over each other.

547
00:32:28,820 --> 00:32:29,820
So try to stay impartial.

548
00:32:29,980 --> 00:32:33,060
But yeah, I think those three have been quite impressive from what I've seen.

549
00:32:33,060 --> 00:32:37,420
And then, again, going back this fuller stack to come full circle now that we've talked

550
00:32:37,420 --> 00:32:40,300
about where a lot of the needs, all the pain points are.

551
00:32:40,300 --> 00:32:43,460
Where do you guys and Octane sit exactly?

552
00:32:43,460 --> 00:32:45,340
Why do customers find you?

553
00:32:45,340 --> 00:32:48,860
And what's your vision for what you guys want to continue to be?

554
00:32:48,860 --> 00:32:50,860
So let me start with the vision side.

555
00:32:50,860 --> 00:32:53,900
One of our most important missions in the company is to make sure that teams we work

556
00:32:53,900 --> 00:32:56,860
with deploy securely and don't get hacked.

557
00:32:56,900 --> 00:33:01,700
The vision for the company is effectively automating code-level security.

558
00:33:01,700 --> 00:33:05,820
So taking the creation of source code and going all the way up to the deployment of

559
00:33:05,820 --> 00:33:10,860
source code and making sure that we can actually automate a lot of the security processes of

560
00:33:10,860 --> 00:33:16,740
finding vulnerabilities, confirming vulnerabilities, producing remediations for vulnerabilities,

561
00:33:16,740 --> 00:33:22,340
meaning code fixes, and actually creating potential exploit paths relative to each bug.

562
00:33:22,500 --> 00:33:27,100
And if we can surface that all in a really intuitive, easy to understand way to developers,

563
00:33:27,100 --> 00:33:29,020
it speeds up the security process for them.

564
00:33:29,020 --> 00:33:32,300
It adds additional security for their teams.

565
00:33:32,300 --> 00:33:34,420
We're covering more parts of the code.

566
00:33:34,420 --> 00:33:36,420
We're finding more types of bugs.

567
00:33:36,420 --> 00:33:39,220
And it's an additional layer there that's really, really important.

568
00:33:39,220 --> 00:33:42,660
And then going all the way to deployment, we have our security research team that can

569
00:33:42,660 --> 00:33:45,700
perform these point-in-time manual checks for our teams.

570
00:33:45,700 --> 00:33:50,300
But yeah, our long-term vision is to really scale the automation part of it so that our

571
00:33:50,340 --> 00:33:56,340
AI systems are effectively handling 95% of the bulk of vulnerability identification or

572
00:33:56,340 --> 00:33:57,260
remediation.

573
00:33:57,260 --> 00:34:01,540
So yeah, we sit squarely in the pre-deployment security pipeline, which means before you

574
00:34:01,540 --> 00:34:07,340
go live and on every pull request of new code, our software will run all of our AI models

575
00:34:07,340 --> 00:34:10,820
and find vulnerabilities that are introduced relative to code changes.

576
00:34:10,820 --> 00:34:15,220
And we can also run a full code base over Octane so we can do point-in-time checks using

577
00:34:15,220 --> 00:34:16,220
our AI as well.

578
00:34:16,260 --> 00:34:20,700
And we like to try to get teams all the way up to deployment and securely thereafter.

579
00:34:20,700 --> 00:34:24,140
I love the direction you guys are going with the point-in-time checks because I think there

580
00:34:24,140 --> 00:34:29,940
was a period where security was very static in terms of manual audits.

581
00:34:29,940 --> 00:34:31,380
There wasn't live monitoring.

582
00:34:31,380 --> 00:34:35,300
And another great insight I heard at one point was you can never be perfect.

583
00:34:35,300 --> 00:34:38,980
You can never completely mitigate the chance that something will happen to your protocol

584
00:34:38,980 --> 00:34:40,260
or what you built.

585
00:34:40,260 --> 00:34:44,820
But the way that these adversaries think is they're trying to target the weak elk in the

586
00:34:44,820 --> 00:34:45,300
pack.

587
00:34:45,300 --> 00:34:49,020
So you're trying to make your protocol just look like one that is robust and people are

588
00:34:49,020 --> 00:34:51,820
going to say, oh, I don't want to look to attack this protocol.

589
00:34:51,820 --> 00:34:55,580
They're working with Octane and they've had their manual audits and they have live monitoring.

590
00:34:55,580 --> 00:34:59,140
And if you can do that, you position yourself in a really good place.

591
00:34:59,140 --> 00:35:01,380
You actually become less of a target.

592
00:35:01,380 --> 00:35:07,380
If you have a bug bounty that's live and you've gone through all these crazy security procedures,

593
00:35:07,380 --> 00:35:11,980
adversaries don't want to spend as much time trying to hack you because there's other easier

594
00:35:11,980 --> 00:35:13,300
targets out there.

595
00:35:13,300 --> 00:35:14,300
It's really interesting.

596
00:35:14,300 --> 00:35:18,220
And I think not only does it help from a pure security perspective, but it also helps

597
00:35:18,220 --> 00:35:19,540
from an optics perspective.

598
00:35:19,540 --> 00:35:21,820
Like you're saying, I completely agree with you.

599
00:35:21,820 --> 00:35:26,380
What are your best stories, if you have any, from your experience in and around this area?

600
00:35:26,380 --> 00:35:32,180
One of my favorite stories here is we re-architected a lot of our product this year and we call

601
00:35:32,180 --> 00:35:33,700
it Octane V2.

602
00:35:33,700 --> 00:35:34,820
Why was that?

603
00:35:34,820 --> 00:35:38,500
We re-architected because we realized that there's a much better way to approach the

604
00:35:38,500 --> 00:35:41,220
problem of automatic code detection.

605
00:35:41,220 --> 00:35:46,300
So we took a lot of the learning from our first version of Octane and then just rewired

606
00:35:46,300 --> 00:35:47,380
the whole thing.

607
00:35:47,380 --> 00:35:50,100
And it's now exponentially more performant.

608
00:35:50,100 --> 00:35:53,340
Our false positive rate is extremely, extremely low.

609
00:35:53,340 --> 00:35:59,420
Somewhere between three to maybe 7% of findings that will ever surface are invalid findings.

610
00:35:59,420 --> 00:36:04,380
The rest are either true valid issues that people are either going to change their code

611
00:36:04,380 --> 00:36:08,420
based on, or they're going to not change their code and accept the risk.

612
00:36:08,420 --> 00:36:11,220
And both of those two are important things for teams to understand.

613
00:36:11,220 --> 00:36:15,740
So when we re-architected this whole version of our product, about like two months ago,

614
00:36:15,740 --> 00:36:19,780
I had the founder of Covenant, which is a protocol that's going live pretty soon.

615
00:36:19,780 --> 00:36:22,960
He came to me and he was like, I'm happy to make a bet with you.

616
00:36:22,960 --> 00:36:27,660
If Octane can find a vulnerability in this code that's higher critical, I will work with

617
00:36:27,660 --> 00:36:29,500
you guys on an annual contract.

618
00:36:29,500 --> 00:36:32,020
And the code was already audited once as well.

619
00:36:32,020 --> 00:36:34,300
So we ran Octane same day.

620
00:36:34,300 --> 00:36:38,260
And after it was done about 30 to 45 minutes later, I had him up and I'm like, hey, we

621
00:36:38,260 --> 00:36:39,260
found the crit.

622
00:36:39,260 --> 00:36:40,260
And he's like, no way.

623
00:36:40,260 --> 00:36:43,060
So we jumped on the call and I showed him the vulnerability.

624
00:36:43,060 --> 00:36:44,540
He was like, yeah, this is real.

625
00:36:44,540 --> 00:36:47,460
And then there were a bunch of other vulnerabilities he found as well, which he really appreciated

626
00:36:47,460 --> 00:36:49,200
and led to a bunch of code changes.

627
00:36:49,200 --> 00:36:50,700
But I thought that was really impressive.

628
00:36:50,700 --> 00:36:55,560
That was one of the ways that I've seen the system be incredible in terms of value creation

629
00:36:55,560 --> 00:36:56,560
for someone.

630
00:36:56,560 --> 00:37:01,180
And that was a really good story that happened in August or September this year, 2025.

631
00:37:01,180 --> 00:37:02,960
Yeah, I love that sales cycle.

632
00:37:02,960 --> 00:37:03,960
You guys should run that.

633
00:37:03,960 --> 00:37:08,080
I think I'd heard about a web builder, something like that, who would just send DMs and be

634
00:37:08,080 --> 00:37:10,720
like, hey, this is what your newly designed website looks like.

635
00:37:10,720 --> 00:37:11,720
Would you like it?

636
00:37:11,720 --> 00:37:13,760
And they started to convert a lot of business.

637
00:37:13,760 --> 00:37:14,760
It works.

638
00:37:14,760 --> 00:37:18,120
Our goal with a lot of these teams is to show, not tell.

639
00:37:18,120 --> 00:37:22,520
So we often put teams on a one or two week pilot period where we scan their code using

640
00:37:22,520 --> 00:37:23,520
Octane.

641
00:37:23,520 --> 00:37:24,520
We walk them through the findings.

642
00:37:24,520 --> 00:37:29,440
And we bring in our manual security team to provide guidance on remediations if they have

643
00:37:29,440 --> 00:37:30,560
further questions.

644
00:37:30,560 --> 00:37:34,960
And then they end up finding the vulnerability so useful that pretty much every single time

645
00:37:34,960 --> 00:37:37,280
they want to be an annual customer with us.

646
00:37:37,280 --> 00:37:41,360
And it's really quick for them to get that value too, because it's just a scan on our

647
00:37:41,360 --> 00:37:42,360
side effectively.

648
00:37:42,360 --> 00:37:44,440
So that process works really well.

649
00:37:44,440 --> 00:37:46,800
We have a very high conversion rate off that.

650
00:37:46,800 --> 00:37:47,800
Very cool.

651
00:37:47,800 --> 00:37:49,980
Gio, it's been great getting to hear your insights.

652
00:37:49,980 --> 00:37:53,280
What should people do if they want to get in touch with you guys, if they want to explore

